  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><para class="os-raise-text-bold"><emphasis>Students will complete the following questions to practice the skills they have learned in this lesson.</emphasis></para>
<para>For 1 - 4, use the following passage:</para>
<para>Harrison works two part time jobs. One at a gas station that pays $11 an hour and the other is IT troubleshooting for $16.50 an hour. Between the two jobs, Harrison wants to earn at least $330 a week. How many hours does Harrison need to work at each job to earn at least $330?</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
  <item>Let \(x\) be the number of hours he works at the gas station and let \(y\) be the number of (hours he works troubleshooting. Write an inequality that would model this situation.</item>
</list>
<list>
  <item>\(11x + 16.5y ≥ 330\)</item>
  <item>\(16.5x + 11y ≥ 330\)</item>
  <item>\(11x + 16.5y ≤ 330\)</item>
  <item>\(16.5y + 11x &gt; 330\)</item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> \(11x + 16.5y ≥ 330\)</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="2" type="enumerated">
  <item>Which graph correctly represents the inequality from the previous question?</item>
</list>

<list type="enumerated">
  <item>
    <figure><media alt="A graph is shown of a line crossing the \(y\)-axis at 10 and the \(x\)-axis at 15. The region above the line is shaded.&#10;&#10;"><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/294638ace54307c8532ae9b7e61b7592e5658bff"/></media></figure></item>
  <item>
    <figure><media alt="A graph is shown of a line crossing the \(y\)-axis at 30 and the \(x\)-axis at 20. The region above the line is shaded."><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/87e1101b4522dee5e1e0993a25a52826195a1fe8"/></media></figure></item>
  <item>
  <figure><media alt="A graph is shown of a line crossing the \(y\)-axis at 20 and the \(x\)-axis at 30. The region above the line is shaded."><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/cac6f8950a8494af2e45b865c8f0680e1d5dec1b"/></media></figure></item>
  <item>
    <figure><media alt="A graph is shown of a line crossing the \(y\)-axis at 30 and the \(x\)-axis at 20. The region above the line is shaded."><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/b83cde708f1c213773bc1c086f6b6216de8b9b07"/></media></figure></item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer: </emphasis>C  
<figure><media alt="A graph is shown of a line crossing the \(y\)-axis at 20 and the \(x\)-axis at 30. The region above the line is shaded."><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/cac6f8950a8494af2e45b865c8f0680e1d5dec1b"/></media></figure></para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="3" type="enumerated">
  <item>Which of the following ordered pairs is a solution to the inequality?</item>
</list>
<list>
  <item>\((10, 20)\)</item>
  <item>\((25, 2)\)</item>
  <item>\((15, 9)\)</item>
  <item>\((3, 17)\)</item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> \((10, 20)\)</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="4" type="enumerated">
  <item>Which of the following is true about the solution you found in the previous question?</item>
</list>
<list>
  <item>The \(y\)-coordinate represents how many hours Harrison will work at the gas station.</item>
  <item>The \(x\)-coordinate represents how many hours he works at the IT job.</item>
  <item>The solution represents a work schedule that earns him more than $330 per week.</item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> The solution represents a work schedule that earns him more than $330 per week. </para>
<para>For 5 - 8, use the following passage: </para>
<para>The doctor tells Maya she needs to exercise enough to burn 500 calories each day. She likes to run which burns 15 calories per minute or bike which burns 10 calories per minute.</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="5" type="enumerated">
  <item>If x is the number of minutes that Maya runs and y is the number of minutes she bikes, find the inequality that models the situation.</item>
</list>
<list>
  <item>\(15x + 10y &lt; 500\)</item>
  <item>\(15x + 10y ≥ 500\)</item>
  <item>\(15x + 10y &gt; 500\)</item>
  <item>\(15x + 10y ≤ 500\)</item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> \(15x + 10y ≥ 500\)</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="6" type="enumerated">
  <item>Which graph correctly represents the inequality from the previous question? </item>
</list>

<list type="enumerated">
  <item>
    <figure><media alt="A graph is shown of a line crossing the \(y\)-axis at 25 and the \(x\)-axis at 35. The region above the line is shaded."><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/67cef90769a04988a386016df6355ca380527017"/></media></figure></item>
  <item><figure><media alt="A graph is shown of a line crossing the \(y\)-axis at 50 and the \(x\)-axis at 20. The region above the line is shaded."><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/1f74cf13af60e3b751b57399cd5429407af3dc3b"/></media></figure></item>
  <item><figure><media alt="A graph is shown of a line crossing the \(y\)-axis at 50 and the \(x\)-axis at about 33. The region above the line is shaded."><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/aa4147c08db38611cf8a1b18d1a2cef2c8280e61"/></media></figure></item>
  <item><figure><media alt="A graph is shown of a line crossing the \(y\)-axis at about 33 and the \(x\)-axis at 50. The region above the line is shaded."><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/604ed5ed1c5194bd9ba02f87b233c524d0317b25"/></media></figure></item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer: C</emphasis></para>
<figure><media alt="A graph is shown of a line crossing the \(y\)-axis at 50 and the \(x\)-axis at about 33. The region above the line is shaded."><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/aa4147c08db38611cf8a1b18d1a2cef2c8280e61"/></media></figure><list class="os-raise-noindent" start="7" type="enumerated">
  <item>Which of the following ordered pairs is a solution to the inequality?</item>
</list>
<list>
  <item>\((5, 40)\)</item>
  <item>\((15, 30)\)</item>
  <item>\((20, 15)\)</item>
  <item>\((25, 10)\)</item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> \((15, 30) \)</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="8" type="enumerated">
  <item>Which of the following is true about the solution you found in the previous question?</item>
</list>
<list>
  <item>The \(x\)-coordinate represents the number of minutes that Maya bikes.</item>
  <item>The \(y\)-coordinate represents the number of minutes that Maya runs.</item>
  <item>The solution represents a workout which allows Maya to burn at least 500 calories.</item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> The solution represents a workout which allows Maya to burn at least 500 calories. </para>
<para>For 9 - 11, use the following passage: </para>
<blockquote>
  <para>Armando’s workouts consist of kickboxing and swimming. While kickboxing, he burns 10 calories per minute and he burns 7 calories a minute while swimming. He wants to burn at least 600 calories each day.</para>
</blockquote>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="9" type="enumerated">
  <item>If \(x\) is the number of minutes that Armando will kickbox and <emphasis>y</emphasis> is the number of minutes he will swim, find the inequality that will help Armando create a workout for today.</item>
</list>
<list>
  <item>\(7x\;+\;10y\;\geq\;600\)</item>
  <item>\(7x\;+\;10y\;&lt;\;600\) </item>
  <item>\(10x\;+\;7y\;&gt;\;600\) </item>
  <item>\(10x + 7y ≥ 600\)</item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer: </emphasis>\(10<emphasis>x</emphasis> + 7<emphasis>y</emphasis> ≥ 600\)</para>
<para>10. Which graph correctly represents the inequality from the previous question?</para>
<list type="enumerated">
  <item>
  <figure><media alt="A graph is shown of a line crossing the \(y\)-axis at 60 and the \(x\)-axis at about 85. The region above the line is shaded."><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/670d0e89069078484ebf1c83f05dd7378a652fa8"/></media></figure></item>
  <item>
  <figure><media alt="A graph is shown of a line crossing the \(y\)-axis at about 85 and the \(x\)-axis at 60. The region above the line is shaded."><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/ef2df1f537ff9c2c5fc2e38cde2e09a755329ff9"/></media></figure></item>
  <item>
  <figure><media alt="A graph is shown of a line crossing the \(y\)-axis at about 85 and the \(x\)-axis at 30. The region above the line is shaded."><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/5280fbc39926d829507b525ef86d6c253039d937"/></media></figure></item>
  <item>
  <figure><media alt="A graph is shown of a line crossing the \(y\)-axis at 30 and the \(x\)-axis at about 85. The region above the line is shaded."><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/6518c9f5bae426e69157f3fd23c44ff921792c4c"/></media></figure></item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> B
<figure><media alt="A graph is shown of a line crossing the \(y\)-axis at about 85 and the \(x\)-axis at 60. The region above the line is shaded."><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/ef2df1f537ff9c2c5fc2e38cde2e09a755329ff9"/></media></figure></para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="11" type="enumerated">
  <item>Which of the following ordered pairs is a solution to the inequality? </item>
</list>
<list>
  <item>\((20, 70)\)</item>
  <item>\((10, 70)\)</item>
  <item>\((30, 40)\)</item>
  <item>\((40, 20)\)</item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> \((20, 70)\)</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="12" type="enumerated">
  <item>Which of the following is true about the solution you found in the previous question?</item>
</list>
<list>
  <item>The \(x\)-coordinate represents the number of minutes that Armando swims.</item>
  <item>The \(y\)<emphasis>-</emphasis>coordinate represents the number of minutes that Armando swims.</item>
  <item>The solution represents a workout which allows Armando to burn at least 500 calories.</item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> The \(y\)-coordinate represents the number of minutes that Armando swims.</para></section>
